title: Romans: Introduction – Who is Paul? and a Logical Argument
description: An introduction to the Epistle to the Romans: who Paul is, when and why he wrote, and the diatribe form of his logical argument.
output: lessons/romans/romans-intro.html

# Folds romans-intro-paul1.html (Who is Paul? Week 1) and romans-intro-diatribe.html (A Logical Argument) into one deck.

=== Who is Paul?
--- Romans: Who is Paul? [title]
overline: Week 1
heading: ROMANS: Who is Paul?
sub: A Visual Study Guide
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the study. Tap any gold reference to read the verse. The Contents button lists every section.

--- The Epistle to the Romans
lead: The Book of Romans is the longest and most doctrinally significant of Paul’s letters. It serves as a systematic explanation of the gospel of grace, written to a church Paul had not yet visited.

--- Who, When & Where?
panel: Author | Paul (Scribe: Tertius)
panel: When | AD 57–58 (3rd Journey)
panel: Where | Written from Corinth

--- Why Did Paul Write It?
point: 1. | To systematically explain the gospel of grace.
point: 2. | To unify Jewish & Gentile believers in Rome.
point: 3. | To prepare for his upcoming visit.
point: 4. | To gain support for missions to Jerusalem & Spain.

--- Quick Facts
panel: Longest Epistle | 16 chapters, 7,111 words.
panel: A church Paul did not plant | Only letter to a church Paul didn’t plant.
panel: Old Testament quotes | Quotes the Old Testament ~60 times.

--- A Letter Steeped in Scripture
lead: Romans is deeply connected to the Old Testament, with ~60 quotes. Paul draws heavily from foundational texts to build his theological argument for the gospel.
p: This is an estimated breakdown of the primary sources for these quotes, demonstrating Paul’s reliance on the Psalms, Isaiah, and Deuteronomy to explain how Jesus is the fulfillment of God’s promises.
stat: 20 | Psalms
stat: 15 | Isaiah
stat: 10 | Deuteronomy
stat: 15 | Other Old Testament Books
note: Number of quotes (estimated)

--- Who is Paul & Why Does He Matter?
lead: The author of Romans is one of the most pivotal figures in history. His life was defined by a dramatic transformation, from a zealous persecutor of the church to its most influential apostle.
panel: Saul of Tarsus: The Persecutor | A Pharisee from the Tribe of Benjamin. Zealously persecuted the early church in Jerusalem. Was present at the martyrdom of Stephen (Acts 7).
panel: The Conversion: The Damascus Road | Occurred around AD 35. A great light from heaven and the voice of Jesus. Christ’s words: “Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?”

--- Paul’s Ministry & Legacy
lead: Following his conversion, Paul spent years in preparation before embarking on a ministry that would establish the theological foundations of Christianity and spread the gospel across the Roman Empire.
panel: Epistles Written | **13** books of the New Testament establishing Christian theology.
panel: Role | **The Apostle to the Gentiles** ([[Romans 11:13]])
panel: Preparation | **3** years spent in Arabia, likely receiving teaching directly from Christ.

--- A Life in Motion: Paul’s Timeline
kicker: From the Damascus Road to Romans
step: ~ AD 35 | **Conversion.** Encounters Christ on the Damascus Road.
step: AD 35–38 | **Preparation in Arabia.** Spends 3 years in preparation.
step: AD 38 | **Begins Ministry.** Returns to Damascus and begins to evangelize.
step: AD 46–47 | **1st Missionary Journey.** Travels with Barnabas to Cyprus and Galatia.
step: AD 49 | **Jerusalem Council.** Defends the gospel of grace (Acts 15).
step: AD 50–52 | **2nd Missionary Journey.** Travels to Philippi and Corinth.

--- A Life in Motion: Paul’s Timeline (continued)
kicker: From Romans to Rome
step: AD 53–57 | **3rd Missionary Journey.** Spends 3 years in Ephesus.
step: AD 57–58 | **Writes Romans.** Writes the epistle from Corinth.
step: AD 58 | **Arrested in Jerusalem.** Imprisoned after delivering the offering.
step: AD 60–62 | **House Arrest in Rome.** Preaches for 2 years; writes Prison Epistles.
step: ~ AD 67 | **Martyrdom.** Martyred in Rome under Nero.

=== A logical argument
--- Romans: A Logical Argument [title]
overline: Understanding the Form and Format of the Epistle
heading: Romans: A Logical Argument
sub: In the epistle, or letter, to the Romans, Paul is using a dialectical format called a **Diatribe**.

--- Dialectic: Discovering Truth Through Logical Exchange
lead: In an argument presentation, **dialectic** is a method of reasoning that seeks to discover truth through the exchange and logical reconciliation of opposing ideas.
p: It is a process of critical analysis and dialogue (or even a single structured presentation) that aims for a higher, more comprehensive understanding rather than simply “winning” a debate.

--- Diatribe: A Vigorous, Conversational Moral Discourse
lead: A **diatribe** (Greek *diatribē*, meaning “pastime” or “discourse”) was a form of popular moral discourse or lecture among Greek and Roman philosophers.
p: It is an oral or written presentation that uses a vigorous, conversational style to explore ethical questions and find truth.

--- The Diatribe: Dialogical Approach and Moral Exhortation
panel: Dialogical Approach | It often employed a dialogue format with an imaginary or hypothetical opponent (**interlocutor**). It used a question-and-answer structure, rhetorical questions, and responses to anticipated objections. This was an extension of the Socratic method, designed to guide students or listeners to a better understanding of a philosophical point.
panel: Moral Exhortation | The primary goal was not simply debate, but moral instruction and persuasion. It aimed to expose contradictions in the audience’s presuppositions and help them overcome false and biased ideas they held.

--- The Diatribe: Accessible Style and a Single Theme
panel: Accessible Style | The language used is often colloquial, direct, and sometimes informal. It utilized colorful, everyday imagery, anecdotes, and historical examples to make complex philosophical ideas accessible to a general audience.
panel: Focus on a Single Theme | These discourses typically centered on a single ethical or philosophical theme. Paul’s overall theme is the **election of grace**, the understanding that the Gentile believers in Christ were as much God’s chosen people and equal part of the Kingdom as Jewish believers, and the implications of salvation by grace through faith for the believer’s life.

--- The Connective Tissue
kicker: Inferential and Explanatory Words
lead: Words like **“For,” “Because,” “Wherefore,”** and **“Therefore”** are not prepositions; they are **logical connectors** or **inferential markers**. They are essential transitional phrases that structure the debate, allowing the reader to trace Paul’s logic from premises to conclusions.
panel: 1. Explanatory (Causal) Markers: “For” & “Because” | These introduce the **reason** or **cause** behind a previous statement. They tell the reader, “Here is the evidence that supports what I just said.”
panel: 2. Inferential (Conclusive) Markers: “Wherefore” & “Therefore” | These introduce the **consequence** or **conclusion** drawn from the preceding facts. They tell the reader, “Based on everything I just proved, this is the logical outcome.”

--- The Connective Tissue: Markers at a Glance
table: Marker Type | Key Words
tr: **Explanatory (Premise)** | **For** (*gar*), **Because**
tr: **Inferential (Conclusion)** | **Therefore** (*dio*, *oun*, *ara*), **Wherefore**
p: **Function in the argument.** Explanatory markers introduce the **reason**, **evidence**, or **bedrock doctrine** that justifies the preceding claim. Inferential markers introduce the **logical outcome** or **major implication** of the premises just established.

--- Paul’s Logical, “If/Then” Structure
lead: Paul’s diatribe uses a logical, “if/then” structure.
p: He teaches the bedrock doctrines of faith as the building blocks for the main argument.
step: IF | Everyone (Jew & Gentile) is under sin.
step: THEN | Salvation is by grace through faith.
step: THEREFORE | Gentile believers are equal in the Kingdom.
step: HOW THEN | Should believers live?
